  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWater vapor imagery early this afternoon shows a pair of upper\nlevel lows in Southern Canada and very dry air advecting into\nthe northeast on the South side of the lows. Given the cooler\nair mass aloft and warm waters on Lake Ontario, there have been\nsome lake effect rain showers and these will persist into the\nearly evening when the cold air advection weakens. Surface high\npressure is building in and with nights starting to get a little\nlonger and some boundary layer moisture in place, valley fog\nwill likely develop in the overnight hours, lasting through\nsunrise, across the Twin Tiers of NY and PA. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe weather pattern will turn more active late in the weekend and \ninto early next week as a broad upper level trough digs into the \nNortheast US. With a large ridge out across the intermountain\nwest, NW flow will dominate in the Northeast under the trough.\nThis will maintain some higher wind shear, with ensemble bulk\nshear means near 25 to 35 knots Monday through Wednesday. PWATs\nwill be up near or over 1.5 inches and mean CAPE getting up near\n500 J/kg indicates there will be moisture and fuel. Once again \nit is looking like an active severe pattern, though right now \nCAPE and Shear means are lower than the last few weeks.",
